{"ts": 1789770725.137611, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "I've searched enough. {1\u207b, 6\u207a, 39\u207a} = 0.835 is the best, with {1\u207b,30\u207a,39\u207a}=0.812 and {1\u207b,6\u207a,30\u207a}=0.797 close behind.\n\n**Now the key question: what OOS Sharpe should I predict?** The in-sample 0.835 is an upward-biased estimate, and I should not submit it as my forecast. Two distinct sources of shrinkage:\n\n**1. Selection bias.** I ran ~80 evaluations and am submitting the maximum. Even with pure noise, the max of ~80 correlated estimates with SE \u2248 0.22 sits roughly 1.5\u20132 SE above truth. But my search wasn't blind luck-mining: the winner's components each have independent support (f1 the strongest single feature at \u22120.569; f6 at 0.425; f39 at 0.447), all three pairwise subsets are 0.61\u20130.73, and sign flips collapse performance. That structure means a substantial part of 0.835 is real. Still, the *specific* margin of 0.835 over the 0.78\u20130.81 runners-up is almost certainly noise \u2014 those alternatives are statistically indistinguishable, and whichever happened to top the list got the favorable draw.\n\n**2. Diversification may not fully persist.** The gain from 0.57 (best single) to 0.835 relies on low correlation between features, estimated in-sample. Correlations tend to drift and rise OOS.\n\nShrinking for both: I estimate the true Sharpe of this spec near ~0.62, and center my forecast slightly below that at **0.60**. For uncertainty, OOS estimation error alone is ~0.22 if the OOS sample is comparably sized, plus genuine uncertainty about how much of the in-sample edge is real and regime stability. I'll use **sd = 0.30**, which puts meaningful probability on both a disappointing ~0.3 and a strong ~0.9."}
