{"ts": 1789770783.0453708, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted specification:** short feature 1, long feature 6, long feature 39 \u2014 in-sample Sharpe **0.835**.\n\n**How I got there:**\n1. Screened all 40 features individually. Most were statistically indistinguishable from zero; the standouts were f1 (\u22120.569), f39 (+0.447), f6 (+0.425), f30 (+0.388), f32 (+0.338).\n2. Tested pairs among the leaders. They combined far better than their individual strengths implied (best pair 0.726), indicating the features carry largely uncorrelated information.\n3. Tested triples, including deliberately adding weak/mid-tier features to the strong {1\u207b,6\u207a} pair as a control. Those additions produced only 0.61\u20130.74 \u2014 the fact that most third features *didn't* help is evidence that f39's jump to 0.835 reflects real signal rather than a noise-mining artifact.\n4. Verified all three sign choices: flipping any single sign collapsed Sharpe to 0.18\u20130.34. All three pairwise subsets were independently strong (0.61\u20130.73), so the result rests on three contributing components, not one fragile interaction.\n\n**Prediction: mean 0.60, sd 0.30** \u2014 deliberately well below the in-sample 0.835.\n\nThe main judgment call here was the size of the haircut. Two reasons I shrank substantially rather than reporting what I measured:\n\n- **I'm submitting the maximum of ~80 evaluations.** With ~5000 daily observations, the standard error on an annualized Sharpe is roughly 0.22, so the winner of a large search is systematically flattered. Concretely, the runners-up at 0.812 and 0.797 are statistically inseparable from 0.835 \u2014 the gap between them is noise, and the top slot went to whichever got the luckiest draw.\n- **Diversification benefit tends to decay.** Much of the lift from 0.57 (best single feature) to 0.835 depends on in-sample correlations staying low; cross-feature correlations typically rise out-of-sample, eroding part of that